"20260728193105356166_b4a80d81", "20260728193105427824_695c9e3a"], "reason": 
"The museum entry explicitly dates the ring to the 16th–15th century BC and 
situates it in the Mycenaean period [20260728193024823162_30e81c51 → 
20260728193104790928_b913f3b8], while also describing broader Mycenaean art 
tendencies (focus on religious symbols, paucity of daily-life scenes and 
emotional expression) [20260728193022469452_e56c47e7 → 
20260728193105246160_93e1180f], providing stylistic context. The same text 
frames the ring within the typology of Minoan/Mycenaean signet rings, and its 
bibliography cites A. Sakellariou’s CMS volume ‘Die minoischen und mykenischen 
Siegel des Nationalmuseums in Athen’ (CMS I, 180) 
[20260728193022824186_bcd15971 → 20260728193105284417_d1f5ba53], which is 
explicitly identified as a key catalog reference for classifying the ring 
within the corpus of seals [20260728193105284417_d1f5ba53]. Iconographic and 
religious interpretations are presented in multiple forms: the scene has been 
interpreted as mythological, based on its narrative structure and the ship with
high stern and cabin reminiscent of the Akrotiri Flotilla Frieze, and in that 
context the couple has been tentatively identified as Theseus and Ariadne 
fleeing after the killing of the Minotaur [20260728193022605937_d693a4e9; 
20260728193022632804_51004870 → 20260728193104960880_97358541]. The same museum
text reports an alternative non‑mythological reading that sees the scene as a 
plain farewell before battle, comparable to the later Warrior Crater from 
Mycenae [20260728193022662256_f6641720 → 20260728193105025641_4ee06978]. It 
also interprets the scene as important because it seems to depict love or 
affection, a theme considered largely absent from Mycenaean art, drawing on 
broader observations about Mycenaean iconography 
[20260728193022521163_7e6f56d5; 20260728193022549435_fc397097 → 
20260728193105119944_11c18836; 20260728193105246160_93e1180f]. Beyond the 
museum text, a 2013 scholarly article, ‘Some Observations on the Iconography of
the “Ring of Nestor”’, published in the Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di 
Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente, is explicitly described as 
analyzing the ring’s iconography within the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates, revisiting key iconographic features and comparative 
material to reassess the ring’s significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art 
[20260728193020037754_d5c8ed64; 20260728193020511727_20cf7e06; 
20260728193020932758_a380286a → 20260728193105356166_b4a80d81]. The same 
