'b1-ebf0e906', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring held in the
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, where it forms part of the Collection
of Prehistoric Antiquities and is catalogued under inventory number P 6209.[5] 
It originates from the so‑called Tiryns Hoard, a group of Late Bronze Age 
objects said to come from Tiryns in the Argolid, and is dated on stylistic 
grounds to the 16th–15th century BC within the Mycenaean period.[5] The ring’s 
bezel is engraved with a detailed scene showing two couples, one beside a 
building and another near an oared ship, which has attracted attention because 
it appears to depict affection or farewell between human figures, a subject 
that is relatively uncommon in surviving Mycenaean art.[5]\n\nThe object 
acquired the conventional name “Ring of Nestor” in modern scholarship through 
comparison with Homeric descriptions of precious rings and signet jewellery and
through its perceived narrative quality, though no ancient inscription links it
directly with the legendary king of Pylos.[1][2] Since the early 20th century 
it has been discussed alongside other gold signet rings from Aegean contexts, 
including material from the Tiryns Hoard and finds from chamber tombs in 
Boeotia, as well as with rings from the “Griffin Warrior” tomb at Pylos that 
illustrate close connections between Minoan and Mycenaean elite culture.[2][3] 
The ring’s iconography and attribution have been the subject of sustained 
scholarly debate, ranging from mythological readings of the scene and 
suggestions of an afterlife context to questions about the authenticity of the 
object and its place in the corpus of Aegean seals.[1][2] More recent work has 
treated the ring as evidence for the circulation of Minoan‑style imagery and 
religious concepts among Mycenaean elites and for the use of signet rings as 
markers of status and power.[1][3][5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e32', 'e33', 'e34', 
'e39', 'e6', 'e7', 'e8', 'e9', 'e10', 'e11', 'e12', 'e18', 'e19', 'e20', 'e21',
'e22', 'e23', 'e24', 'e25', 'e26', 'e27', 'e28', 'e1', 'e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5'],
'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 
'b2-7f74be0d', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is a solid gold signet ring whose
hoop and bezel were designed to function both as personal ornament and as a 
sealing device.[5] Museum catalog data describe it simply as a “gold signet 
ring”, indicating that the engraved bezel could be impressed in clay or wax, in
keeping with the wider Aegean practice of using gold and other materials for 
seals.[5] The recorded diameter of the hoop is about 1.9 cm, while the bezel 
measures approximately 2 × 3.4 cm, placing it among the larger Late Bronze Age 
finger rings known from the Aegean.[5] Comparative measurements of ancient and 
later rings assembled in early scholarship show inner diameters for Greek 
examples around 18.5 mm, suggesting that the Ring of Nestor would have fitted 
an adult wearer and that its size was consistent with a high‑status 
object.[2]\n\nThe bezel carries a finely engraved figural scene divided into 
two main groups. On one side, a couple stands at the entrance of a building, 
while on the other, a second couple is depicted on what has been interpreted as
a shoreline indicated by rocks and an oared ship with a high stern and 
cabin.[5] The figures are rendered in low relief with details that are not 
always visible to the naked eye, including subtle hand gestures that curators 
have identified as signs of close relationship between the left‑hand pair: the 
woman’s fingers wrap around the man’s arm and his free hand reaches toward her 
face.[5] Both men and women wear garments typical of Late Bronze Age Aegean 
fashion, with the women in long pleated skirts and the men in Minoan‑style 
loincloths (zōma), and swords and spears are shown hanging at the men’s 
waists.[5] These costume and weapon details situate the ring within the broader
repertoire of Mycenaean and Minoan imagery, where similar skirts, loincloths 
and armament appear on seals and figurines.[2][5]\n\nStylistically, the 
engraving has been compared to other Late Minoan intaglio work, in which heads 
are treated in a summary way but garments can be elaborately detailed, such as 
skirts with multiple flounces and the depiction of small girls in contemporary 
fashion.[2] The ship with its distinctive stern and cabin has been linked by 
museum commentary to the “Flotilla” fresco from Akrotiri on Thera, dated to the
16th century BC, which shows comparable forms of vessels in a coastal or 
maritime setting.[5] The ring’s composition and technical execution have 
therefore been used to place it within a network of high‑quality Aegean seal 
engraving that combines Mycenaean funerary or narrative concerns with Minoan 
iconographic and stylistic conventions.[1][2][5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e32', 
'e14', 'e17', 'e34', 'e38', 'e37', 'e15', 'e16', 'e35', 'e33', 'e1', 'e3', 
'e4', 'e5'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 
'b2-7f74be0d', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is associated by museum records 
with the Tiryns Hoard, a group of gold and other objects attributed to Tiryns 
in the Argolid and incorporated into the holdings of the National 
Archaeological Museum in Athens.[5] The hoard is catalogued within the 
Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, and the ring bears the inventory number 
P 6209.[5] The museum presentation emphasizes the ring’s role within a 
Mycenaean assemblage that also includes other signet rings, seals and 
jewellery, although the precise circumstances of the hoard’s discovery are not 
detailed in the brief catalog entry.[5]\n\nThe modern scholarly label “Ring of 
Nestor” derives from early 20th‑century discussions of Aegean gold rings and 
bead‑seals, in which a “royal treasure of gold rings and bead‑seals in the same
metal” from a Mycenaean rock‑cut tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia was 
first described under that title in the Journal of Hellenic Studies.[2] In that
account, the author states that the treasure was found in 1915 by a peasant in 
a chamber tomb cut into the rock near the village of Dombrena, and that 
information about the findspot and associated tombs came in part from W. A. 
Heurtley of the British School at Athens, who reported seeing three chamber 
tombs with dromoi in an olive grove on the Akropolis height of Thisbe.[2] 
Additional objects from the same group of tombs, including bronze spearheads, a
short sword of late Mycenaean type, a bronze razor and a small perforated 
double‑axe head of iron, were noted, though not all could be securely linked to
the chamber containing the gold treasure.[2] Glass‑paste beads, faience plaques
and other jewellery elements from the assemblage were dated to the period of 
the Dimini jewels, identified with early Late Minoan III B, providing a 
chronological framework for the hoard.[2]\n\nThe later identification of the 
Tiryns Hoard ring in Athens with the Ring of Nestor rests on correspondence 
between museum catalog data and published descriptions in scholarship on Aegean
seals.[5] In the museum’s online presentation, the ring is explicitly linked to
the Tiryns Hoard and to earlier cataloging work by A. Sakellariou in the 1964 
volume “Die minoischen und mykenischen Siegel des Nationalmuseums in Athen” 
(CMS I, 180), which integrated the ring into the standard corpus of Minoan and 
Mycenaean seals.[5] The ring’s present location is given as the Exhibition of 
Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28, indicating that it is 
displayed within a broader narrative of Mycenaean and Minoan art and that its 
provenance from the Tiryns Hoard is part of that curatorial context.[5]', 
'evidence_refs': ['e32', 'e6', 'e7', 'e8', 'e9', 'e10', 'e11', 'e12', 'e40', 
'e33'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}]})
